{
  "requirement": "Each bundled skill's description MUST state when to use it in terms of the user's task. It MAY name another bundled skill to use instead where an agent could confuse the two. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:\n\n:::{sites} skills\n```yaml\nlanguage: markdown\npaths: [assets/skills]\nrule:\n  kind: minus_metadata\nmatch:\n  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"\nno_match:\n  - \"# Example\\n\"\n```\n:::",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Site query** (`requirements.cli.skill_conventions/skills`): It selects the five `assets/skills/*/SKILL.md` files: `habeas`, `habeas-requirements`, `habeas-evidence`, `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` and `habeas-refactor`. A search for `---` front-matter fences under `assets/skills` found exactly these five files. The `references/*.md` files carry no front matter and are not selected. `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skills_are_five_skill_directories_each_home_to_its_topics]` also confirms the skill directories on disk. The match snippet has front matter and the no_match snippet has none, so the \"recognized by its front matter\" condition has a snippet on each side. I found no missed places and no out-of-scope places.\n\n**Obligations:**\n- Each description MUST state when to use it in terms of the user's task. This is the one obligation assessed here. `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skill_descriptions_say_when_to_use_them]` loops over all five skills. It first checks the front matter: one `name` and one non-empty `description` per skill, at most 1024 characters. It then asserts that each description starts with a skill-specific task-triggered \"Use …\" phrase, such as \"Use when adopting Habeas in an existing codebase\" and \"Use during design sessions and before changing code, tests, or requirement text\". These are task-based triggers.\n- A description MAY name another bundled skill to use instead. A MAY imposes nothing to check. The test still verifies that any `habeas-*` name in a description is a bundled skill. It also asserts the specific \"use X instead\" pointers for the skills that could be confused.\n\n**Gaps:** The test is marked `{test}` for the subject. I found no unmarked relevant tests and no unaddressed obligations. The violation queries were not separately at issue. The test itself also covers this requirement for each skill.",
  "references": "4fbf9a3072386612109271b7cf55673834816c2318c1c11463d91326147378e5",
  "inputs": [
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.cli.skill_conventions",
      "matches": [
        "requirements.cli.skill_conventions"
      ],
      "text": [
        "::::{req} skill_conventions",
        "Each bundled skill's description MUST state when to use it in terms of the user's task. It MAY name another bundled skill to use instead where an agent could confuse the two. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:",
        "",
        ":::{sites} skills",
        "```yaml",
        "language: markdown",
        "paths: [assets/skills]",
        "rule:",
        "  kind: minus_metadata",
        "match:",
        "  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"",
        "no_match:",
        "  - \"# Example\\n\"",
        "```",
        ":::",
        "::::"
      ]
    },
